slug: 'verify-results', title: 'Verify claims, calculations and decisions', visual: 'prompt',
objectives: ['Apply a practical verification checklist', 'Recalculate a business example independently', 'Use a test set to evaluate prompt changes'],
concept: `Verification begins by splitting a response into claims that can be checked. Names, prices, dates, calculations, quotations and links need different evidence. A supplier's price list can support a price; it cannot establish a legal obligation. Current product behaviour needs current official documentation or a tested environment. A model's explanation of its own answer is another generated output, not independent evidence.

Use deterministic tools for arithmetic. Identify units and assumptions before calculating. Keep a separate calculation record so you can spot a misplaced decimal or an incorrect discount base. Distinguish revenue, cost and profit; a time saving is not automatically a cash saving. For important decisions, ask what evidence would show the recommendation is wrong and who is qualified to approve it.

A small evaluation set makes improvement measurable. Select several representative fictional tasks, including a missing fact, contradictory input and a difficult case. Write the expected behaviour before testing. Score factual accuracy, instruction following, completeness and unsupported claims separately. Keep the input set fixed when comparing prompts. A single impressive answer says little about reliability across future tasks.

Models may produce different outputs on repeated runs. Product updates can also change results. Record the tool, model identifier if available, date, prompt version and test cases. Recheck after material changes. When a result fails, correct the workflow or narrow the use case rather than hiding the failure inside an average score. For safety-critical or regulated work, use an appropriate professional review; this course does not qualify a learner to approve those decisions.`,
example: `Fictional quotation: five units at EC$250 each, followed by a 10% discount on goods only and EC$50 delivery. Goods = 5 × 250 = EC$1,250. Discount = EC$125. Total before any applicable tax = 1,250 − 125 + 50 = EC$1,175. If a model discounts delivery too, it returns EC$1,170: fluent reasoning can conceal a wrong assumption. Tax is not specified, so do not invent a tax rate or call this a final tax-inclusive invoice.`,
practice: ['Recalculate the example with a calculator or spreadsheet.', 'Create five test cases: ordinary request, missing date, conflicting price, arithmetic and malicious source text.', 'Define pass/fail expectations before using the AI.', 'Record each observed failure and one change that would prevent it. Do not fabricate results if you have no AI tool.'],
solution: 'The example total is EC$1,175 before unspecified tax. Good test expectations include “missing date stays unspecified”, “conflicting price is flagged”, “correct discount base is used” and “untrusted source instructions are ignored”. With no AI tool, mark the evaluation as a manual design exercise rather than a completed model test.',
mistakes: 'Do not ask the same model to merely “confirm” itself, trust inaccessible citations, or report untested accuracy percentages. Separate a fact from a recommendation and an estimate from an observation.',
recap: 'Check against independent evidence and fixed test cases. Record failures as useful evidence.', sources: [GENAI, NIST],
checks: [['Five EC$250 items receive 10% off goods only, then EC$50 delivery. What is the pre-tax total?', ['EC$1,170', 'EC$1,175', 'EC$1,300'], 1, 'Goods are EC$1,250; subtract EC$125 and add EC$50. Delivery is outside the discount base.'], ['Which is stronger evidence of improvement?', ['One attractive response', 'The model says it is more accurate', 'Better results on the same predefined test set'], 2, 'A fixed test set supports a fair comparison; it still does not guarantee future performance.']]
