slug: 'content-and-media', title: 'Create useful writing and visual briefs', visual: 'prompt',
objectives: ['Turn a business goal into a content brief', 'Separate factual copy from creative choices', 'Check visual accuracy, rights and accessibility'],
concept: `Content starts with the reader's need and the next useful action. A product announcement, a customer instruction and a social advertisement have different goals. Write a brief that names the audience, channel, approved facts, tone, length and call to action. Keep factual constraints separate from creative choices so a revised style does not accidentally change a price or condition.

For visual work, describe the purpose, composition, subjects, format and required factual elements. Ask for Caribbean representation without reducing people to stereotypes. Technical diagrams need exact labels and relationships, so use structured drawing tools or carefully authored SVG rather than trusting a generated picture of a circuit, network or chart. Generated imagery is useful for illustration, but it can contain impossible equipment, misspelt text or misleading detail.

Check accessibility as part of production. An informative image needs alternative text describing its meaning in context. A chart needs labelled units and an equivalent table or textual account. Decorative images can have empty alt text. Avoid placing essential instructions only inside a picture. Captions and transcripts make audio or video more usable; machine-produced captions also require checking.

Creative output still needs rights and truth checks. Confirm that you have permission to use source photographs, branding, music and personal likenesses. Do not fabricate endorsements or before-and-after results. Label illustrative charts and synthetic scenes where readers might otherwise assume evidence. Review each platform's current publishing requirements when you actually publish; this course does not hard-code changing dimensions or advertising rules as universal facts.`,
example: `Fictional brief: “Create a square illustration for a small-business workshop on organising digital files. Show an adult Caribbean business owner using a laptop in a bright workspace. Leave room for a headline. Do not show real customer records or brand logos.” The factual copy separately states the approved workshop date, location and booking method. A human checks those facts and the image before release.`,
practice: ['Choose a fictional workshop and write a six-field content brief.', 'Draft a short announcement with one clear call to action and no unsupported claims.', 'Write an image brief and a separate alt-text description for its intended meaning.', 'List the rights, accuracy and accessibility checks needed before publishing.'],
solution: 'A useful announcement says what the workshop teaches, who it is for, the approved date and how to book. It does not claim “guaranteed business growth”. Suitable alt text might describe a business owner organising files on a laptop if that supports the article. If the image is purely decorative and the adjacent text carries all meaning, empty alt text may be more appropriate.',
