slug: 'safe-workflows', title: 'Design a reliable assisted workflow', visual: 'ai',
objectives: ['Separate drafting from external actions', 'Design validation, approval and failure handling', 'Estimate value without inventing savings'],
concept: `A workflow is a sequence of responsibilities, not merely a long prompt. Define its trigger, inputs, processing, checks, approval, action and record. For example, an approved service note can trigger a draft reply; a manager checks it; a separate system sends the approved message. Keep these boundaries visible. A language model generating a reply is not evidence that the reply was delivered.

Connected tools increase capability and risk. An incoming email or webpage may contain misleading instructions. Restrict the sources and actions available to a workflow. Use least privilege: a drafting tool does not need permission to delete records or transfer money. Validate structured output before it reaches another system. Reject unknown fields, missing identifiers and unsupported values rather than treating well-formed JSON as a correct decision.

Plan for retries and failures. Repeating an action such as sending an invoice can create duplicates. Use a stable operation identifier and check whether the action already succeeded before retrying. If the outcome is unknown, reconcile it rather than automatically repeating it. Keep an audit record of the approved input, reviewer, action and outcome without exposing unnecessary personal data.

Measure a pilot honestly. Record total handling time including checking and corrections, not only model response time. Compare equivalent tasks and include unsuccessful cases. Calculate usage cost and supervision effort. Stop or narrow the workflow when errors exceed the acceptable boundary. A simpler template may outperform AI for a fixed repetitive message. There is no benefit in maintaining a more complex system that saves no useful time or increases mistakes.`,
example: `A fictional repair business tests ten appointment drafts. Manual handling averages six minutes; assisted drafting plus review averages four minutes. The observed saving is two minutes per tested task, or twenty minutes across those ten tasks. This is illustrative arithmetic, not a measured V79 result. It does not justify claiming a 33% payroll reduction or projecting the same saving to every case.`,
practice: ['Draw a seven-stage workflow for an appointment draft.', 'Mark the boundary between untrusted input and trusted instructions.', 'Specify a stop condition, approval owner and duplicate-prevention rule.', 'Design a pilot log with task, time, correction, cost and outcome columns. Leave observations blank until a real test occurs.'],
solution: 'Trigger: approved booking record. Input: minimum approved facts. Draft: constrained model response. Validate: facts and format. Approve: coordinator. Act: send once using a stable booking-message ID. Record: actual delivery status. If the send times out with unknown outcome, check provider status before retrying. Pilot results should include review and correction time.',
mistakes: 'Do not connect unrestricted actions to untrusted messages, treat a timeout as proof of failure, or equate a faster draft with an equivalent business saving.',
recap: 'Keep permissions narrow, approvals explicit and outcomes observable. Measure the whole task.', sources: [NIST, GENAI],
checks: [['An invoice send times out and the result is unknown. What is the safest next step?', ['Send three more copies', 'Check whether the original action succeeded before retrying', 'Mark it delivered'], 1, 'Reconciliation avoids duplicates and false success claims.'], ['Which time belongs in a pilot comparison?', ['Only model generation time', 'Only the fastest run', 'End-to-end time including review and correction'], 2, 'The business pays for the whole process, including supervision and failures.']]
